iew {value} in the National Vulnerability Database"}), "publicSource":({"title":"Source: public data, not Dependabot", "detail":"The configured GitHub token cannot read the Dependabot alerts for this repo, so these rows come from its public dependency graph matched against the OSV advisory database. That means no dismissals are honoured, nothing is deduplicated the way Dependabot does it, and there is no runtime versus development split — so these counts run higher and are not comparable with the counts on a Dependabot repo. Advisories that carry no severity rating at all are counted as Unknown rather than being graded down to Low.", "unresolved":"{count} dependencies were declared as a version range the dependency graph never resolved. They are excluded rather than guessed at."}), "rollup":({"explain":"The estate view reads all {count} repos, which takes around a minute and a few hundred requests. Individual tabs are much cheaper, so this one is on request rather than automatic.", "load":"Load all security data", "loading":"Loading…", "dependabot":"From Dependabot", "publicData":"From public data", "repoCount":"{count} repos", "failed":"Could not read: {repos}"}), "scopeNote":({"shell":"This package lives in rancher/dashboard rather than a repo of its own. Dependabot records the lockfile each alert was raised against, and shell has one, so these rows are the alerts filed against shell/yarn.lock and nothing else in the monorepo.", "components":"This package lives in rancher/dashboard and no longer has a lockfile of its own, so its transitive dependencies resolve through the workspace root and Dependabot files them against the root yarn.lock along with the whole monorepo. These rows are everything filed against pkg/rancher-components, plus the root lockfile alerts for packages that its package.json declares under dependencies or peerDependencies. That covers its direct dependencies; anything transitive pulled in since the lockfile was removed cannot be told apart fro